  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "If $f(t)=A\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{5})}^{t}$ and $f(3)=4,$ then $A=$",
  "worked_solution": "We substitute $t=3$ and $f(t)=4$ into the expression for $f(t)$ and solve for $A,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =A\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{5})}^{3} \\\\ 4 & =A\\cdot (\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{5}^{3}}) \\\\ 4 & =A\\cdot \\frac{1}{125} \\\\ 4\\cdot 125 & =A \\\\ 500 & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A=500.$",
